# Controllers design

Controllers holds the Fearless manager: a desktop program that keeps a set of project folders, compiles them, runs their mains, and connects them to the desktop and to various IDEs, for now just Eclipse.
Next to it, and independent from it, Controllers also holds:
- `fearlessPluginProject`: the Eclipse plugin showing the projects of a manager inside Eclipse, with the content assist of `suggest`.
- `agentTools`: a library driving the real pointer and keyboard, for GUI tests of any program.
- `benchmarks`: speed measures of Fearless programs against the runtime.

The manager behaves the same on every system: it does not adapt to the conventions of the system it runs on, and when the system does not offer what the manager needs (a display, a file system feature the manager relies on), the manager stops with a fatal error saying so.
This is the only concept of 'fatal error'. The manager should attempt to discover if we are unsupported as early as possible, but may discover fatal errors while running. For example, the HD could stop working or the OS could start reporting we are headless from now on.
Thus, the following are not fatal errors: calling the manager program with wrong arguments either wrong format or arguments pointing to non existing folders
The problems of one project (bad file names, a bad icon, a missing folder, a failing compile) are not fatal errors, they are part of normally running the manager.

A fatal error is shown in a dialog listing the jobs that are running;
closing the dialog ends the manager and every job it runs.
When the manager ends for the fatal error, this happens in order:
-The dialog shows. Nothing below happens until the dialog has been closed.
-All the running jobs are terminated. Wait for confirmation of the termination.
-Delete the manager folder.
-Removes all the associations the manager took.

Everything the manager is asked to do either happens or is refused with a message naming what was refused and why; nothing is silently skipped.

## Program, manager folder, owner

A manager is one program folder, `fearlessManaged<version>` (`fearlessManaged<version>.app` on macOS), for example `fearlessManaged0_001`.
Its manager folder is `fearless<version>`, next to the program folder, on every system. The manager folder holds everything the manager remembers; deleting it makes the manager start from nothing. That is, a starting manager detecting no manager folder should do the needed clean up, including icons and files associations.
A manager folder that can not be created is a fatal error naming it.
Substantial association related failures are also fatal errors (that is, we can not take/release the fearless or some of the fapp/ffile associations).

## Starting the 'single' manager.
Starting the manager program leaves one message in the manager folder. There could be multiple parallel attempts to start the manager, and the 'single' manager could be already running. The manager that is recognized as the 'single' manager handle the messages, while the other manager processes terminate just after leaving their message in the manager folder.

The manager program takes zero arguments or one argument:
- zero arguments: the message is empty (show the window);
- one argument starting with `Base16:`: the message is the text whose UTF-8 bytes are the hex digits after `Base16:`;
- one other argument: a path, made absolute against the working folder of the manager program; the message is the absolute path as a tagged text (register the folder, or the folder holding the file).
A folder whose name starts with `Base16:` is given as `./Base16:...`.
Every run of the manager program writes its message file. A run that is not the single manager then ends; a run that becomes the single manager applies its own message file like every other message file.
If a manager is called with bad arguments, it will leave a message "invalid call xxx" where xxx represents the details, and when the single manager take that message up, it will show the error in a dialog as for all the other dialogs and continue running.
If a manager encounter some (fatal) error but in a set up where the single manager is likely to either be active or become active soon, it just leaves a message "fatal error xxx" about it and end normally. When possible, the single manager is the only thing taking actual actions.
When the single manager handles "fatal error xxx" it does treat it as a fatal error.
If a manager encouter fatal errors likely to prevent a 'single' manager to emerge, it will delete the manager folder and remove all the associations the manager took. By definition there are no running fearless processes to terminate in this case.

The single manager runs until Quit, or until a fatal error. Quit ends the manager and every job it runs.

Closing the window hides it, but we need to make sure something is left graphically to show the continued life of the `single` manager process.
If an OS supports system tray/StatusNotifierItem, this is whay we use.
Clicking it shows the window, and its menu holds Show manager and Quit manager.

On a system with no tray, the manager shows the icon itself.
The jframe is closed and replaced with another jframe: a window of the size of a tray icon, with no title bar and no border, holding only the Fearless icon.
Left click on that window opens shows the manager as for left click on the tray icon.
Right click on that window shows the menu the tray icon would have if supported.
When the manager is 'shown again', the simulated tray icon jframe is removed and the manager jframe is started again.

## Requests and messages

Everything the manager does for a project is a request, and every request is one of the requests below, whether it comes from a message or from GUI interactions.
The single manager applies the requests one at a time, in the order they arrive, each one completely before the next.

A message is the file `messages/<time>-<id>.msg` in the manager folder: `<time>` is the 20 digit count of milliseconds since 1970 when the message was written, and `<id>` is a random UUID. The single manager applies the message files in the order of their names, and deletes each message file after reading it.
Only the manager program writes message files: the manager program writes `messages/<time>-<id>.tmp`, then renames `messages/<time>-<id>.tmp` to `messages/<time>-<id>.msg`, so the single manager never reads a partial message. The single manager deletes every `.tmp` file older than 60 seconds.
Every other program commanding the manager (the Eclipse plugin, a script, a test) runs the manager program with one `Base16:` argument holding the message.
Gui operations are just another way to command the manager:
every gui operation has an equivalent message, and every message is either ill formed or has an equivalent gui operation.
Since all errors are caused by messages and all messages go via the single manager, a program that sends a message gets the potential error via the manager GUI. If the manager gui is not displayed, it is displayed first.

A message is one of:
- empty: show the window;
- one line, a path as a tagged text (see **Info files**): `register` that path;
- a request, of two or three lines: a verb, then the argument of the verb, then for some verbs a third line.

`register` takes a path as a tagged text; every other verb takes the name of a registered project:
- `register <path>`: register the folder (see **Registering a folder**), or the folder a file is in, then select it; a folder already registered is only selected;
- `select <name>`: show the window and select the project;
- `run <name>`: select the project and run it (see **Jobs**); the third line, when present, is the one main to run;
- `compile <name>`: select the project and compile it if it needs compiling;
- `check <name>`: select the project and report why it is invalid, or that it is not;
- `terminate <name>`: select the project and end its job, if any;
- `clean <name>`: select the project and delete its compiled cache;
- `kind <name>`: set the kind of the project to the third line (`idle`, `code`, `data:readOnly`, `data:readWrite`);
- `forget <name>`: forget the project;
- `mains <name>`: set the selected mains of the project to the third line, the mains separated by spaces;
- `link <name>`: set a link of the code project to a data project to the third line: the name of the data project, then `read` or `write`, then the type names the code uses for it, separated by spaces; no type names removes that link;
- `clear <name>`: empty the Output of the project.
- `invalid call <details>` and `fatal error <details>`: written by a run of the manager program that is not the single manager. These two messages have no equivalent gui operation.

Ill formed messages:
- a message of more than three lines;
- a verb that is not one of these;
- a name that is not the name of a registered project (the message lists the registered projects);
- a `register` whose path is blank, names nothing, is the root of a drive, or is the manager folder, anything inside it or a folder holding it;
- a kind that is not one of the four, and a malformed link;
- a request whose result would make `projects.info` invalid (the message is the error of `projects.info`).
- `run` and `compile` of a project that is not code, naming its kind;
- `link` of a project that is not code;
- a job, `clean` or `kind` while the project has a job (see **Jobs**);
- a `kind` change that is not from idle or back to idle.

The window's GUI elements should be designed with good GUI design with the principle of 'discoverability' in mind.
This includes both display elements and buttons and menus allowing to send the messages described above.

## Info files

The files the manager writes for itself and for Eclipse (`projects.info`, `state.info`, `manager.info`) are Info files: text in the Fearless Str allowed character set.
The Info format is a simplified Json format. 
Infos are strings `"..."`, lists `[a, b]`, or objects `{"key": value, ...}` whose keys are distinct strings;
Comments are not supported.
Inside an info string, `\"` is a quote, `\\` a backslash and `\n` a newline; no other escape are present; thus `"\t"` would be an invalid Info.

A text that may hold characters outside the Fearless character set is a tagged text:
- `"Str:.."` then the text, when every character is in the Fearless character set: `"path": "Str:\"C:\\data\\hello\"`;
We use this when the string is a valid Fearless Str. Note `\\` instead of `\\\\` in the example above. The fearless Str is `C:\data\hello` and prints as `"C:\data\hello"` (fearless code that would produce that same string content when parsed). This works since fearless strings do not use `\` for escapes at all.
For example the string `Hello\nWorld` is produced from the fearless code `"Hello"|"World"` and thus it would be encoded as `"Str:\"Hello\"|\"World\""`
- `"UStr:.."` then the text as `UStr.escape` prints it. We use this when the string would not be a valid Str but it would be a valid UStr. Refer to UStr.escape to understand this format.
- `"Base16:.."` then the bytes. This is needed, for example, to represent arbitary file system paths that may not be valid unicode. This encoding should faithfuly represent the bytes that the current file system would be able to interpret as the specific file path.


## Registered projects

The manager folder holds `projects.info`, the record of every registered project: where it is, its kind, the mains the user selected, and what the project may read and write.

`projects.info` is an Info file: an object mapping each project name to its metadata:
```
{
  "hello_world": {
    "path": "Str:C:/data/01_hello_world",
    "kind": "code",
    "mains": ["hello.Hello1", "hello.Hello3"],
    "reads": {"pub": ["Pub"], "notes": ["OldNotes"]},
    "edits": {"notes": ["Notes", "Diary"]}
  },
  "diary": {
    "path": "UStr:\"C:/Users/Jos\".u\"00E9\"+(\"/da\".u\"1F600\")+(\"ta\".u)",
    "kind": "data:readWrite"
  }
}
```
- `path`: the absolute path of the project folder, any path the system allows. Two projects never share a folder, and a project folder is never inside another one.
- `kind`: `idle`, `code`, `data:readOnly` or `data:readWrite`.
- `mains`: the mains the user selected to run, for a code project with several mains.
- `reads` and `edits`: for a code project, the data projects it may read, and those it may read and write, each with the type names the code uses for it. A project in `reads` is `data:readOnly` or `data:readWrite`; a project in `edits` is `data:readWrite`. A data project may be in both: the type names in `edits` read and write, those in `reads` only read; a type name is not in both. A link names at least one type name.

A code project whose links name a missing project, or a project of the wrong kind, or an invalid project, is invalid, and the message says which link and why.

A `projects.info` that is not a valid Info file, or does not hold what is above, is a fatal error pointing at the offending text in the Frontend error message style. 
A manager initiated action should never edit a project info file in a way that is invalid, thus the fatal error above shows either a manager bug or OS/user interference in the manager folder.

The whole file can be edited by hand from the GUI, or via a message naming a file (outside of the manager folder) with the intended new content.
An edit is accepted only if the whole text is valid, and an error points at the offending text in the Frontend error message style.
An accepted edit applies at once: projects it removes are forgotten, projects it adds are registered as they are, every project is checked again.

The manager can deduce from metadata inside the relevant .fearless_out, per project, when it was last compiled and last run.


## Registering a folder

The name of a project is the name of its marker: the only file with the `.fearless` extension at the top of its folder, `<name>.fearless`. A project name follows the rules of a single file name segment of a Fearless project (lowercase letters, digits and `_`, not starting with a digit, not a reserved name such as `con`).

Registering a new folder that is not registered already:
Error if: the folder name is insider an already registered one, or vice versa an already registered project contains the folder name.
Name inference: The name is the name of its marker, when it has exactly one, the name is valid, and no other project uses it.
Otherwise the name is the folder name turned into a valid name: lowercased, every run of other characters turned into `_`, a leading `p` when it starts with a digit, then the first of `name`, `name2`, `name3`... not used by another project. The marker is renamed (or created) to match.
If present, the compiled cache (`.fearless_out`) of the newly registered folder is deleted.
The folder is now in the 'idle' state.

Forgetting a project asks no confirmation, but gives an error if there is an active fearless program running over it.
Forgetting a project removes the project from `projects.info`; nothing in the project folder is changed.

## Project states

Each project is in one of these states, shown as a badge on its tile:
- `code: invalid content`, `data: invalid content`: its folder is missing, its file names are not valid Fearless file names (for code, also its package structure), its top level .fearless file is missing or not the only one, dangling links to data projects this project uses, or the manager can not display this project icon;
- `idle`;
- `data: read only`, `data: read write`;
- `code: not compiled (no cache)`;
- `code: not compiled (cache out of date)`: a compiled cache that does not match the sources (see below);
- `code: compiled`;
- `code: busy`: a job runs.

A code project is out of date exactly when compiling it would compile some package: when the compiled cache of a package does not list exactly the current files of that package, or is older than one of them. Files outside every package (a readme, the marker) never make a project out of date.

The mains of a code project are known when it is not out of date: the concrete mains of the highest rank (could be more then one package).
When reading them finds a package that compiling would compile, the project state becomes out of date; when reading them fails, the project state becomes invalid, with that error and the .fearless_out is cleared. A project whose files change while it is checked is checked again.

While the window is shown, the manager notices changes made on disk by other programs: every three seconds it checks the selected project, and in turn one other project, and updates states, badges, buttons and the Eclipse files, including moving a project in and out of invalid. A project with a running job is checked when the job ends. Every request naming a project checks it.
The manage do not check for changes when the window is not shown. This include the tray, true or emulated, and minimization.

The icon of a project is the only PNG file in the folder `.config/icon` of the project, or, with no such folder, an icon generated from the project name. More than one PNG, or a PNG that can not be read, makes the project invalid.

Kinds change request: from idle to any kind, from any kind back to idle. Changing kind keeps `mains`, `reads` and `edits`: they have no effect while the project is not code, and come back if it becomes code again.

## General Gui shape:
A menu bar at the top. This also includes an OrderBy sorting the icons below by Name, last Modified, last Compiled or last Run.

A simplified 'desktop with icons' either taking the whole space or taking the left space of a vertical divider.
Icons represent registered folders: each shows the project icon with its state badge, and the project name under it.
A busy project (compiling or running) shows an animated badge.
When an Icon is selected, the divider opens and on the right we see the project area.

##Project are contains, top to bottom:

A header: the project icon, the main button, the project name, and
the following buttons:
-The main button; it changes depending on the state of the project to one of those options:
Compile, Run, Run V, Terminate, or Check (for a project that is not code);
It is greyed out when its request would be refused (compiling a project with invalid names)
- The Open docs button (enabled once the mains are known). It opens the docs in the favourite browser.
- The kind buttons: Only "Back to idle" for a project that is not idle;
for an idle project "Become data", "Become editable data" and "Become code". They are greyed out while the project has a job.

For a code project, if there is more then one main
we see "Run 'V'" where V is some arrow down signalling that there is more then just running.
When Run V is pressed, it behaves like a drop-down menu showing the available mains, with a checkbox near their names and a 'Run' button at the end of the checkbox list, disabled if nothing is selected.
  
For a code project whose mains claim shortcuts, after the header we see one icon for each claimed shortcut, representing that shortcut icon.
Clicking on it is equivalent of selecting only that main in the "Run V" and running it (or just running it if it is the only main)


For a data project we see, after the header, a button opening the files in the OS favourite file explorer program.

For a code project, we have a Output collapsible area with vertical scroll bars and max size of 1/2 of the gui height.

For a code project whose mains claim extensions after the console we see a collapsible area with vertical scroll bars and max size of 1/2 of the gui height, one block per main claiming extensions:
  "Extensions this main opens:" (each extension with its icon),
  "Conflicting extensions:" (each extension also claimed by another main, naming that main).

For a data project we see a similar collapsible area with vertical scroll bars and max size of 1/2 of the gui height, listing in editable format the project that can see this data, how and under what aliases.
This shows all the (other) projects this data allows access.
As usual, every edit need to go via the messages and will be validated when received.

For a code project, similar but the data is not editable.
This shows all the data projects giving access to this project. 

For a code project we have Logs; collapsible area with vertical scroll bars and max size of 1/2 of the gui height; showing the log files the mains wrote in .out/logs, with an option to see them in the file explorer.


## Jobs

A project does at most one job at a time: compiling, or running its mains. A request for a job while another one runs, and a `clean` or `kind` while a job runs, is refused with an error dialog as for other user errors.
Everything a job prints goes to the Output area of the project.

- Compile (code projects): a code project with broken links is not compiled: the Output shows why. The compiler runs in its own JVM; its output goes to the Output. `--- compiling <folder> ---` starts it, `--- compile done ---` or `--- compile failed with <code> ---` ends it. A compile that ends with the mains known removes from the selected mains those that are no longer mains.
- Run (code projects): a code project with broken links does not run: the Output shows why. When the project is out of date, the main button compiles; a `run` request compiles and then runs. The mains run are: the only main, when there is one; else the main named by the `run` request; else the selected mains, in the order of the mains. A named main that is not a main of the project is refused, listing the mains. Selected mains that are not all mains of the project are refused, naming those that are not, and are removed from the selected mains. A project with no main, or none selected, says so. Each main runs in its own JVM, one after the other, framed by `--- running <main> ---` and `--- <main> exited with <code> after <N>s ---`. While a main runs, its unit test log is published as a JUnit report every two seconds and when it ends. The report holds the complete records of the log: its lines up to the last newline, without a test case whose closing line is not written yet.
- Terminate ends the job: `--- terminating <job> ---`, then the JVM of the running main or of the compile ends, and no further main of that run starts.


## File associations

On Windows and Linux, at every start, the owner makes its launcher the program opening `.fearless` files, with the Fearless icon, following `FileAssociations.reconcile` of Commons. If reconcile does not work, is a fatal error.

## Eclipse

Connect Eclipse asks for Eclipse: its program, a file next to it, its folder, or the folder Eclipse was unzipped into. The Eclipse folder is the one folder holding `.eclipseproduct`.
The connect eclipse operation uses a file picker and do some heuristics to find the eclipse folder from the chosen file.
The accepted cases are as follows:
-we select a file that is the eclipse folder.
-we select a file that is directly inside the eclipse folder.
-we select a file whose direct parent folder is inside the eclipse folder.
-we select a folder that contains only the eclipse folder.

Plugin design: Eclipse will read 
projects.info and follow up into the .fearless_out files when needed to recover information and display the state.
The manager will need some cooperation to ship to eclipse the running output and the error messages. The manager writing a file and eclipse waiting for it was attempted but it was introducing substantial delays.
Ther manager should be able to open a connection of some sort with the running eclipse and communicate there those fast changing informations.
The compile error/output text should contain some end marker so that eclipse (and the human) knows when it is over.

Eclipse commands the manager by running the manager program with one `Base16:` argument holding the message. If a message ends up in error, the manager shows the error as usual.

The plugin mirrors every registered project into the Eclipse workspace, in the Fearless perspective only, and never writes into a project folder; a project the manager forgets is removed from the workspace. A workspace project named like a registered project that is not its mirror is an error. Every two seconds it reflects the manager's files: the problem in the Problems view, on its file at its line, or on the project while that file does not exist, and the report in the JUnit view. It shows the Output in the Console view, and every run as a process with a Terminate button. Build (`compile` for a code project, `check` for any other), Clean, Run, Terminate, the kind switches of the Fearless menu of a project, New Project (`register`), Show in manager and Forget project are messages to the manager. Content assist is described below.
A missing or malformed file of the manager is an error shown in Eclipse, and the plugin stops following the manager until Eclipse starts again.

## Content assist

Content assist works in a Fearless file of a package folder of a mirrored project, after a dot and on request. It uses the compiled information of the last compile of the project and of the compiled standard library: the api json of every package, the map directives of the project as that compile resolved them, and the text documentation of the package of each proposal. A malformed api json or map, a missing documentation file, and a public type or a method of a type missing from the documentation are errors.

It proposes:
- nothing while the cursor is inside a comment or a string, or touches a name (`ps|`, `Perso|`);
- after a dot, or after a space following an expression, the methods of the type of the expression before the cursor, dot methods and operators alike: dot methods first, then by name and number of parameters, one per name and number of parameters. A method name being typed (`.fl|`, `+|`) keeps the methods starting with it and is replaced by the choice; otherwise the choice replaces the dot, or is inserted at the cursor. An operator is inserted with a space on each side;
- after a lowercase name and a dot (`base.|`), and inside a qualified type name being typed (`base.Li|`, in code or in a `use` directive), the public types of the compiled package that name stands for whose simple name starts with what follows the dot.

Each proposal shows its signature, with simple type names and without reference capabilities, and its documentation.

The text is read with the tokens of the compiler. The expression before the cursor is what follows the last `;`, `,`, `->`, `:` or `'` in the innermost bracket around the cursor, read with the grammar of the compiler: an atom, then posts left to right, where a post without parenthesis takes one atom, and `.m x = e rest` binds `x`. A bracket not closed yet runs to the end of the text. What the compiler would reject is unknown, and unknown has no methods. The type of:
- a type name is that type: a simple name is the alias of a `use` directive of the head file of the package (its only `_rank_` file), else a type of the package; a name without `[..]` has no generics. A package name written in the package, in a qualified name or a `use` directive, stands for the package the `map` directives of the project give it there. A number or a string is a `base.Nat`, `base.Int`, `base.Float` or `base.Str`;
- a name is the type of what binds it. The literals around the cursor bind their self name, or `this` for a top level declaration, the generics of a declaration and of the method holding the cursor, and the parameters of that method: to their written type, else to the type of the parameter of the method the literal implements (the method of that name, or, for a method without name, the only abstract method with that number of parameters not implemented by name in the literal). An earlier `x = e` of the same expression binds `x` to the first parameter of the continuation of its method; inside its own `e`, `x` is unknown;
- a literal is the type it declares, or the type written before it, or else the type expected by the call it is an argument of, or the result type of the method it is the whole body of; the generics of that type still open are bound by the types of the bodies of its methods;
- a call is the result type of the method of that name and number of arguments of the type of its receiver, with the generics of the method bound by the explicit type arguments, then by each argument meeting the type of its parameter, left to right, then by the type expected of the call.

A type declared in the edited file that the last compile does not know has, the method from the union of its supertypes.

## Error management
Errors are present in Coordinator (`Report`, `Violation`) when shared with portableFearless, and in Controllers when unique to the manager.
Controllers must have a similar (`Report`, `Violation`) classification inside it.
